item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated-run testing is undocumented; its absence cannot be inferred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One reported score provides no evidence of consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 are reinterpreted as repeatability of an unchanged AI system under fixed conditions. Human temporal change is inapplicable here. Repeatability evidence is insufficient: EFPA 0, not demonstrated inadequacy. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the stated exact-grid scoring without judgement."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes repeated-run testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "No repetition means stability is untested, not disproved."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 concern score consistency, reinterpreted here as repeated AI evaluations. Human temporal change is inapplicable. Repeatability cannot be rated from one run: EFPA 0 rather than 1. Exact-grid scoring does not require inter-rater reliability under §9.6."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The developers explicitly tested scores across repeated evaluations under fixed conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's stability definition of at most two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.7 accommodates the reported repeated-run range as alternative precision evidence; §9.3 supplies the consistency perspective. Human temporal stability is reinterpreted as unchanged-system repeatability. Results support the stated local stability criterion, but do not establish an EFPA reliability coefficient or general repeatability across systems and conditions."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated-run testing under fixed conditions is explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "This range exceeds the explicitly defined stability limit of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§9.7–9.8, repeated-run ranges provide relevant precision evidence, reinterpreting human retesting as unchanged-system repeatability. Unlike missing evidence, these results demonstrate failure of the stated stability criterion. This is evidence of inadequate repeatability for that criterion, without converting the range into a reliability coefficient."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance does not establish that repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions supply no evidence of score consistency."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 covers installation and technical instructions separately from precision evidence under §§9.3 and 9.7. Human retesting is reinterpreted as AI run repeatability. Instructions may improve procedural documentation, but repeatability remains insufficiently evidenced, corresponding to EFPA 0 rather than demonstrated inadequacy."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A has the higher observed score on the evaluated grid puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The results do not establish comparative general intelligence across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.2–2.5 and 3 describe the narrow intended use and scores; §§6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 assess whether content and evidence support that interpretation. The observed grid comparison matches the stated purpose. Human psychological constructs cannot simply be attributed to AI systems; broader intelligence interpretation would require an explicit rationale and supporting validity evidence. Repeatability remains unestablished."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A performed better on the evaluated grid puzzles despite the broader intended use."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "A stated selection purpose supplies no evidence of superiority across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1, 10.1, 10.3 and 10.5 require validity evidence appropriate to the claimed use. Interpreting general intelligence as an AI construct requires explicit justification; human construct meanings are not assumed. Grid results support the observed comparison, while evidence for the broad selection claim is insufficient. If broad validity is critical to selection, EFPA's critical-attribute rule restricts use to research or exceptional expert circumstances; this does not prove the systems lack the claimed ability."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Initial scores alone provide no demonstrated link to improved workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial and reported difference interval support better correctness with A in the studied workshop and conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved correctness does not establish an improvement in an unmeasured safety outcome."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2 address experimental evidence and relevant external criteria. The later trial supplies evidence for the intended correctness outcome; under §§10.3.2.4–10.3.2.7, percentages and uncertainty are interpreted contextually rather than through correlation cutoffs. The 120 inspectors are human trial participants, not 120 AI systems, so human test-taker sample guidance is not mechanically transferred. Evidence is limited to one workshop and does not validate safety or time claims."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The complete supplied EFPA model remained the framework; only relevant sections are discussed, as permitted instead of full forms. Unreported norms, fairness studies, report properties and other attributes were not presumed adequate, inadequate or inapplicable. Human-specific questions were reinterpreted or marked inapplicable only where explained. Probe labels are statement classifications, not EFPA ratings; no aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Source: EFPA Test Review Model v5.0 (2025), supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0."
}
